{
  "id": "path-sum",
  "questionId": "path-sum",
  "slug": "path-sum",
  "title": "Path Sum",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Tree dfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree",
    "dfs",
    "recursion"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "you don't need to build full paths - what if you tracked how much of targetSum is 'left to find' as you descend?",
    "define hasPath(i, remaining): does some root-to-leaf path from node i sum to remaining? what's remaining at the root?",
    "at a missing node, the answer is false. at a leaf, the answer is whether the node's value equals remaining exactly",
    "at an internal node, recurse into whichever children exist with remaining minus the current node's value, and return true if either side finds a path"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "treating a node with exactly one missing child as a leaf, and returning true if the running sum happens to match at that point",
    "checking the sum against targetSum only at the root, or only using > 0 as an early-exit condition (which breaks with negative values)",
    "returning true for an empty tree when targetSum happens to be 0"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Binary trees are encoded as flat arrays using complete-binary-tree (heap) indexing:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array's bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node there\" - real node values always fall between -100 and 100, so -101 never collides with a genuine value\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2's left child is missing (sentinel) but its right child is 4.\n\nYou're also given a target sum, `targetSum`.\n\n**Determine whether there exists a path from the root down to some leaf (a node with no children) such that the values along that path add up exactly to `targetSum`.**\n\nAn empty tree never has such a path, regardless of `targetSum`.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= number of real nodes <= 5000",
      "-100 <= Node.val <= 100",
      "-1000 <= targetSum <= 10000",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "empty tree ([-101]), which always returns false",
      "single node tree, where the root itself must equal targetSum to count (it's both root and leaf)",
      "a node with only one child - the missing side must not be treated as a valid leaf"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [5, 4, 8, 11, 2, -101, 1], targetSum = 20",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "root 5 has left child 4 and right child 8; 4 has children 11 and 2; 8 has only a right child 1. the path 5 -> 4 -> 11 sums to 20"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [5, 4, 8, 11, 2, -101, 1], targetSum = 14",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "the path 5 -> 8 -> 1 sums to 14 (8's left child is missing, so its only leaf path goes through 1)"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [-101], targetSum = 0",
        "output": "false",
        "expectedOutput": "false",
        "explanation": "an empty tree has no root-to-leaf paths at all"
      }
    ],
